# Ink & Inscriptional Technologies Curriculum Cache
#
# Colloidal carbon black, iron gall redox kinetics, Gutenberg oil varnish, and print-capitalism history.

#>spw_cache_mat_ink
#:cache #!ink_and_inscriptional_technologies
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Model the colloidal physics of carbon black, iron gall chelation redox chemistry, capillary fluid dynamics in paper, and the civilizational history of print-capitalism."
  ~#protocol: "cache.layer/1"
}

^"colloidal_physics_and_ink_chemistry"{
  "carbon_black_and_iron_gall_formulations": .{
    carbon_black_colloidal_stability: "Amorphous carbon nanoparticles (10-100nm) produced by incomplete hydrocarbon combustion; suspended in aqueous vehicle with gum arabic or shellac acting as steric protective colloids; completely inert to chemical oxidation and lightfastness",
    iron_gall_coordination_redox: "Aqueous tannin extraction from oak galls (gallic and digallic acids) reacted with green vitriol (ferrous sulfate FeSO4); writes as soluble, pale Fe2+ gallate; atmospheric oxidation generates insoluble, intensely black-violet Ferric Pyrogallate (Fe3+) coordination polymer indelibly staining paper fibers",
    the_curse_of_ink_corrosion: "Iron gall chemical degradation: oxidation releases sulfuric acid byproduct (hydrolyzing paper cellulose glycosidic bonds) and excess Fe2+ ions catalyzing Fenton reactions (generating destructive hydroxyl free radicals that physically eat through historical manuscripts)",
  }[reg=facet],
  "capillary_fluidics_and_modern_print_rheology": .{
    washburn_capillary_penetration: "Liquid penetration into porous fibrous paper governed by Lucas-Washburn equation: L^2 = (gamma * r * t * cos(theta)) / (2 * mu); balancing dynamic surface tension gamma and viscosity mu to prevent feathering while ensuring rapid absorption",
    thixotropic_printing_varnishes: "Offset printing inks formulated with linseed oil, phenolic resins, and high-boiling petroleum distillates; high tack and yield stress preventing ink misting on high-speed cylinders while shear-thinning during roller nip transfer",
    piezoelectric_and_electrophotographic_inks: "Drop-on-demand inkjet ejecting 1-10 picoliter droplets at 30 kHz via acoustic piezoelectric deformation; laser electrophotographic dry toners (styrene-acrylic polymer micro-beads) electrostatic attraction fused by heated rollers at 200 C",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"applications_in_art_law_and_microelectronics"{
  "documentation_art_and_security": .{
    calligraphic_wash_and_east_asian_ink_painting: "Chinese Shan Shui painting leveraging variable water dilution of pine-soot inksticks across absorbent xuan paper, mastering five shades of black (dry, wet, thick, thin, light)",
    legal_record_immutability: "Iron gall ink's chemical bonding with cellulose fibrils preventing mechanical erasure without visible surface destruction, cementing its status as the legal standard for treaties and charters",
    functional_and_conductive_printed_inks: "Formulating conductive inks with silver nanoparticles and graphene flakes for printed RFID antennas, flexible biomedical sensors, and bank check Magnetic Ink Character Recognition (MICR)",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"world_history_and_the_inscriptional_revolutions"{
  "ancient_monastic_scriptoria_to_gutenberg": .{
    the_four_treasures_of_the_chinese_study: "Han Dynasty development of molded soot inksticks rubbed against stone palettes; bureaucratic imperial civil service examinations and woodblock printing (Tang Dynasty) circulating knowledge centuries before Europe",
    monastic_scriptoria_and_the_preservation_of_letters: "Medieval monks hand-copying biblical and classical texts using goose quills and iron gall ink on parchment and vellum; safeguarding Western literacy through the post-Roman centuries",
    gutenbergs_forgotten_breakthrough_oil_based_ink: "Johannes Gutenberg's (1450s) true innovation beyond movable lead-tin-antimony type: water-based scribal inks beaded and dripped off metal type; Gutenberg formulated a thick, sticky oil-and-pitch varnish (linseed oil, turpentine, soot) that adhered evenly to metal letters and cleanly impressed onto paper",
  }[reg=facet],
  "print_capitalism_and_the_democratization_of_pens": .{
    print_capitalism_and_national_consciousness: "Benedict Anderson thesis: mass-produced vernacular print (Luther's German Bible, pamphlets, daily newspapers) destroying Latin monopoly, forging modern standardized national languages and democratic public spheres",
    the_ballpoint_revolution: "Laszlo Biro (1938) combining quick-drying high-viscosity printer's paste ink with a free-rolling tungsten carbide ball socket; British RAF adopting ballpoints during WWII because pressure changes did not cause them to leak in high-altitude cockpits; ending the era of messy blotters and inkwells",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"wonder_probes"{
  #>wonder_mat_ink_1
  ?["Why did Johannes Gutenberg's invention of movable metal type stall completely until he abandoned scribal water-based ink and formulated an oil-varnish ink?"]{
    #:depth #!materials ~#lens(surface_energy_and_contact_angle_wetting)
    ~#hypothesis: "Movable type was cast from a hydrophobic alloy of lead, tin, and antimony. Water-based iron gall ink has a high surface tension (~72 mN/m) and fails to wet the metal alloy, beading up into disjointed droplets that produce blurred, illegible smudges. Gutenberg solved this through materials science: formulating a thick linseed oil varnish with low surface energy that uniformly wet the metal face and transferred cleanly under mechanical screw pressure to dampened cellulose paper."
    !probe{ "Measure contact angles and transfer efficiency of aqueous iron gall ink vs linseed-oil-soot varnish on 80/15/5 lead-tin-antimony alloy plates." }
    $%[surface_chemistry.alloy_ink_contact_angle_degrees, printing.ink_film_transfer_fraction]
  }
}
